  "outcome": {
    "accounting_complete": true,
    "candidate_text": "\\begin{proof}\nFor every $x\\in[0,1)$, $x^n\\to0$, while $f_n(1)=1$ for every $n$. Thus $(f_n)$ converges pointwise to the function $f$ defined by $f(x)=0$ for $x<1$ and $f(1)=1$. Any uniform limit would also be a pointwise limit, so it would necessarily equal this $f$.\nThe sequence $\\left(f_n\\right)_n$ does not converge uniformly, since given $0<\\varepsilon<1$ and $N \\in \\mathbb{Z}_{+}$, for $x=\\varepsilon^{1 / N}$ we have $d\\left(f_N(x), f(x)\\right)=\\varepsilon$.\nWe can also apply Theorem 21.6: the convergence is not uniform since $f$ is not continuous.\n\\end{proof}",
    "cost_usd": null,
